# How a release works

This page documents the release chain of `[[ dist_name ]]`, from commit
message to GitHub release. The steps to follow are in
[Contributing](CONTRIBUTING.md#releasing). It is the chain of
canvodpy-extensions, so the package keeps its history and tags when it
moves there.

A release here is a git tag and a GitHub release. **Nothing is published
to PyPI or Zenodo from this repository**: the `canvod-*` names on PyPI and
the canVODpy records on Zenodo belong to the canVODpy maintainers, who
publish a package once it is part of canvodpy or canvodpy-extensions.

---

## 1. Conventional commits

Commit messages follow `type(scope): subject`, for example
`fix([[ short_name ]]): handle an empty input`. The commit-msg hook
(installed by `just hooks`) runs commitizen's check and rejects other
messages. The messages become the changelog, so mark breaking changes
(`feat!:` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer).

---

## 2. Versions, changelogs, and tags

| Recipe | What it does |
|--------|--------------|
| `just versions` | Prints the version of the package |
| `just changelog` | Prints the package's changes since its last release |
| `just release [[ dist_name ]] 0.2.0` | Runs the package's tests, sets the version (`major`, `minor`, `patch`, or a version number), writes the package's `CHANGELOG.md`, and makes one release commit |
| `just tag` | On an up-to-date `main`, tags the package's version and pushes the tag |

**Changelog.** `packages/[[ dist_name ]]/CHANGELOG.md` is written by
[git-cliff](https://git-cliff.org) (`cliff.toml`) from the commits that
changed `packages/[[ dist_name ]]/`. `just release` adds the new section at
the top and leaves the earlier sections as they are. Edit the new section
in the release pull request where an entry is wrong or missing: the draft
release takes its notes from it.

**Tags.** A release is the tag `[[ dist_name ]]-v<version>`, for example
`[[ dist_name ]]-v0.2.0`. `just release` refuses a version whose tag exists
already. `just tag` refuses to tag unless `main` matches `origin/main` and
the changelog has a section for the tag, so a tag always points at a
merged release commit.

---

## 3. Draft releases

`.github/workflows/release.yml` runs when a tag `canvod-*-v*` is pushed
[% if not ci_enabled %](here: only by hand, `gh workflow run release.yml --ref <tag>`,
since the workflows are off; see [Contributing](CONTRIBUTING.md#continuous-integration))[% endif %]:

1. Reads the package and version from the tag and stops if the tag names no
   package under `packages/` or doesn't match the package's version.
2. Takes the release notes from the tag's section of the package's
   `CHANGELOG.md`, and stops if there is none.
3. Adds a link that opens the release notebook in molab, if the tagged
   commit has `release-notes/<package>-v<version>.py`.
4. Creates a **draft** release titled `<package> <version>` (a pre-release
   for `a`, `b` and `rc` versions). Review it and publish it by hand.

### Release notebooks

A minor or major release gets a marimo notebook that shows what is new and
how to use it: `release-notes/<package>-v<version>.py`, made from
`release-notes/_template.py`. It pins the release it describes, has one
section per change that users notice, and runs on canVODpy's demo data
(`canvodpy.demo_data()`) where it can. CI checks its format only
(`just check-release-notes`). Commit it on the release branch and add it
to `release-notes/README.md`. A patch release needs no notebook.

---

## Complete example

```bash
git switch main && git pull origin main
just changelog
git switch -c chore/release-[[ dist_name ]]-0.2.0
just release [[ dist_name ]] minor
# commit release-notes/[[ dist_name ]]-v0.2.0.py here
git push --no-follow-tags -u origin chore/release-[[ dist_name ]]-0.2.0
gh pr create --fill
[% if ci_enabled %]
# merged once CI passes, then on main:
git switch main && git pull origin main
just tag
# draft release "[[ dist_name ]] 0.2.0" appears: review, publish
[% else %]
# merged once `just check` and `just test` pass, then on main:
git switch main && git pull origin main
just tag
gh workflow run release.yml --ref [[ dist_name ]]-v0.2.0
# draft release "[[ dist_name ]] 0.2.0" appears: review, publish
[% endif %]
```
